From 1d0aa440c190004fc6049d870ce769d11b45c8e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Maher <chris@mahercode.io>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:44:13 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] perf(deepseek-v4.1): prefetch the next prefill chunk's disk
 Engram rows

With SPARK3_ENGRAM_ASYNC=1 the reader thread reads a step's disk Engram
rows beside the forward, but Engram layer 1 (the second of 40) needs them
almost at once: on three DGX Sparks at TP3 a 4,096-token prefill step
still idles about 80 ms waiting on them (profiled; it was 194 ms before
the async path).

SPARK3_ENGRAM_PREFETCH=1 (needs SPARK3_ENGRAM_ASYNC=1) reads them a step
early. When a batch is one request that is still prefilling, the model
runner passes its next chunk (the prompt is fully known, so the next
chunk is the next token budget or the rest). After this step's gates are
released, the reader thread hashes that chunk on the host (a numpy copy
of b12x's Engram hash, checked equal to b12x's CPU oracle) and reads its
rows into a second set of mapped staging planes. The next step binds its
decode to that set; the reader compares every real row ID with the
prefetched ones and skips the read only on an exact match, otherwise it
reads the real rows into the same set as before. The rows the decode sees
are always the rows for the real IDs; only their timing changes.

The two sets alternate, each reader write waits for the decode that last
read its set, and all kernel launches stay on the main thread. The second
set and the host hash tables are set up on the main thread before any gate
is queued; if that fails, prefetch turns off for the process. A failed
prefetch read logs once and the next step reads as usual. Dummy and warmup
steps drop any pending prefetch, and a final chunk short enough to run
padded through a CUDA graph is not prefetched (its padded hash count would
never match). The second set costs one more staging
allocation per Engram table (about 25.5 MiB each at 4,096 tokens).
Off by default. Debug switches: SPARK3_ENGRAM_PREFETCH_CORRUPT=1 flips
one prefetched ID so every step takes the re-read path, and
SPARK3_ENGRAM_PREFETCH_VERIFY=1 re-reads the real rows on every hit and
compares them byte for byte with the prefetched set (a mismatch is logged
and re-read).

Signed-off-by: Christopher Maher <chris@mahercode.io>
